# Command menu

Use Command Menu for searchable actions and links. Its global shortcut is `⌘K` or `Ctrl+K` by default; this example disables it to avoid conflicting with docs search.
Search results retain their group headings and rank the most relevant group first.

Add `groups` to an item for a searchable next step.

<DocsExample title="Command Menu" source={commandMenuExampleSource} htmlSource={commandMenuExampleHtmlSource}><CommandMenuExample /></DocsExample>

## Keyboard navigation

<DocsTable columns={keyboardColumns} rows={keyboardRows} minWidth="var(--ds-sizing-lg)" />

Modified clicks and middle-click open links in a new tab and keep the menu open. They also run `onselect`; opening a link through the browser context menu does not.

## Item schema

- `bind:open` controls visibility.
- `groups` accepts groups with `label` and `items`.
- `placeholder`, `emptyText`, and `label` customize the input, empty state, and accessible dialog name.
- An item accepts `id`, `label`, `description`, `icon`, `href`, `shortcut`, `keywords`, `disabled`, `action`, `groups`, and `placeholder`.
- `groups` opens a searchable next step. `placeholder` optionally changes the search prompt for that step.
- `icon` accepts a registered Aesir icon name and renders it before the item label.
- `href` renders the item as a native link. Without `href`, the item renders as a button.
- `description` adds secondary text. `keywords` participate in filtering without being displayed.
- `itemContent` optionally replaces the default item markup while preserving the menu's filtering, selection, and keyboard behavior.
- `shortcut` accepts a single key, a key sequence such as `['G', 'W']`, or a modifier combination such as `['⌘', '↵']`. Item shortcuts run while the menu is open. A focused search input never intercepts an unmodified single-key shortcut, so normal typing keeps working.
- `onselect` runs after an enabled button or link item is selected, including new-tab link interactions.
- `shortcut={false}` disables the global keyboard shortcut.
- `showFooter={true}` shows the optional keyboard hints.

## Custom surfaces

Use `title` with `headerMeta` for a persistent header. The `content` snippet replaces only the results list and receives the current `query`, `currentPage`, and `close` callback. The `footer` snippet replaces the keyboard hints and receives the same context. This supports settings panels and rich previews without changing the default grouped-command behavior.

When `content` is present, the input uses `searchbox` semantics and Command Menu stops intercepting list-navigation keys. Keyboard behavior inside the custom surface belongs to its controls.

The HTML element uses the same nested `groups` structure through JSON attributes or JavaScript properties.
